1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Eight stations, eleven two-way streets.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Made one-way badly, the map breaks: nothing can get back to A.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
One station reaching all is not enough. Which maps admit a choice that works both ways?

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
This map does. The lesson gives the test, the construction and the proof.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Two triangles joined by one street: that street is a bridge.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Point the bridge either way and one side is cut off.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
One bridge is enough to kill every strong orientation.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
No bridge is necessary. Whether it is sufficient is the hard direction.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
An ear is a path that starts and ends on ground already built.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
B-E-F-D: both ends built, both inside stations new.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
An ear may be one edge, or a closed path that is a cycle.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Two rules hold in every ear: no edge twice, no old ground inside.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
An ear decomposition writes all the edges as a list of ears.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The first ear is a cycle, because nothing is built yet.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Every later ear has both ends in the ears before it.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The definition is not yet a theorem. The theorem says who has one.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Start anywhere: find a cycle and call it ear one.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
From a built station, follow unused edges until you come home.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Ear three, then ear four, until no edge is left.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Four ears, eleven edges, each edge in exactly one ear.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Why must the walk come home? Count the unused edges at a new station.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
No built ear touches it, so all its edges are unused, and there are at least two.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
So there is always another unused edge to leave by: it cannot stall.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
With a bridge the same walk dies at the far end of it.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
One ear with k edges brings k-1 new stations.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
So the number of ears is fixed: edges minus stations plus one.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Eleven minus eight plus one is four: four ears, as found.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The bowtie gives two. The complete graph on four stations gives three.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Now give every ear a direction.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Ear one becomes a directed cycle: every station on it reaches every other.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Each later ear becomes a directed path between two built stations.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Robbins, 1939: a strong orientation exists exactly when connected and bridgeless.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The ears prove the theorem; a search builds the orientation.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Tree edges point away from the root; the others point back up.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
On a bridgeless map the result is strongly connected.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Pointing every edge from the earlier-discovered end is a different rule, and it fails.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Two different questions: how many edges must fail, and how many stations.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
The bowtie is bridgeless: every edge lies on one of its two cycles.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Its second ear is closed: both ends are the shared station.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
An open decomposition needs no cut station: Whitney, 1932.

41
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,500
A disconnected map is out, even with no bridge anywhere.

42
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:15,000
A tree is out: no cycle, so no first ear.

43
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:22,500
One station and no streets is in, by convention: nothing to reach.

44
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:30,000
The theorem promises an orientation, not only one.

45
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:37,500
Step one: is the map connected?

46
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:45,000
Step two: look for a bridge, by deletion or by the low-number test.

47
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,500
Step three: a bridge means no, and the cut is the reason.

48
00:05:52,500 --> 00:06:00,000
Step four: otherwise orient by search and check it by walking from every station.

